WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.960
A dominant allele shows up in heterozygotes as well as in homozygotes.

2
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.780
So why does it not spread, generation by generation, until almost everyone shows it?

3
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:20.160
That was the question put to the mathematician G. H. Hardy in nineteen oh eight.

4
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.140
His answer was no.

5
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.540
It became the reference point for population genetics.

6
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.560
Picture every egg and sperm in a population thrown into one pool.

7
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.380
The pool holds copies of two alleles, M and N. Call the fraction of copies that are

8
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:42.980
M, P. Call the fraction that are N, Q. With only two alleles, P plus Q equals 1.

9
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:49.180
In a sample of 1,000 people, counting copies gives p of 0.5425.

10
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:54.273
Now make each new zygote by drawing one egg and one sperm at random.

11
00:00:55.113 --> 00:00:57.613
The chance of drawing M from both is p x p.

12
00:00:58.293 --> 00:01:00.353
A heterozygote can arise two ways.

13
00:01:01.273 --> 00:01:04.193
M from the egg and N from the sperm has chance pq.

14
00:01:04.953 --> 00:01:06.573
The reverse has chance qp.

15
00:01:07.453 --> 00:01:09.353
These are the Hardy-Weinberg proportions.

16
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:14.033
MM is p², squared, MN is 2pq, and NN is q².

17
00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:15.033
squared.

18
00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:17.877
Now count the M copies in the next generation.

19
00:01:18.877 --> 00:01:20.897
Their fraction is p² + pq.

20
00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:23.577
That equals p(p + q).

21
00:01:24.477 --> 00:01:27.217
Since p + q is 1, the answer is simply p.

22
00:01:27.977 --> 00:01:32.697
The allele frequency has not moved, and one round of random mating restores these

23
00:01:32.697 --> 00:01:34.757
proportions, whatever they were before.

24
00:01:34.757 --> 00:01:35.297
For more information, please visit www .mooji .org for more information.

25
00:01:35.297 --> 00:01:35.850
For more information, please visit www .mooji .org.

26
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.810
The population is large, mating is random at this locus, and there is no selection,

27
00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.250
mutation, migration or drift.

28
00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.190
Real populations break these assumptions.

29
00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:51.390
Hardy-Weinberg is a null model: the expectation to compare data against.

30
00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.830
In our sample, the observed counts sit close to the expected ones.

31
00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:01.530
The chi-square is 0.22, far below the critical value of 3.84.

32
00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:05.750
With one degree of freedom, that means no evidence of departure.

33
00:02:06.883 --> 00:02:10.243
Dominance describes how alleles act inside one person.

34
00:02:11.183 --> 00:02:13.223
It says nothing about how common they become.

35
00:02:14.063 --> 00:02:18.023
To change how common an allele is, something has to act on the population.

36
00:02:19.143 --> 00:02:23.323
Keep this rule. With nothing acting, allele frequencies stay put.
